    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d646">

                <pb facs="0835" n="823" sameAs="frus:frus1946v07/pg823"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">761.67/5–746: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Turkey</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Wilson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Ankara</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1946-05-07T10:00:00+02:00">May 7, 1946—10
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received May 8—6 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>516. Secretary General Foreign Office who represented Turkey at recent
                    liquidation meeting League of Nations, says that while USSR was not present at
                    Geneva its shadow darkened atmosphere throughout meeting. Heads all delegations,
                    except, of course, Soviet satellites, expressed views to him to effect their
                    countries eager cooperate restoration normal peace time activities but hamstrung
                    by Soviet policies and attitudes. They all asked Erkin what Turkey would do <pb
                        facs="0836" n="824" xml:id="pg824"/> if attacked by USSR.<anchor n="42"
                        xml:id="d646-f1"/> He replied Turkey would fight. Some delegates expressed
                    scepticism, pointing out Turkey was small country with small army, and could not
                    hope resist. Erkin replied that if Turkey doomed disappear as result Soviet
                    aggression chances for rebirth independent Turkey much greater if she resists
                    and goes down honorably rather than disappear as result attempting appeasement
                    and becoming Soviet satellite.</p>
                <p>Erkin said he felt his conversations at Geneva helpful in making clear Turkey’s
                    position to representatives other countries.</p>
                <p>Sent Dept, repeated Moscow as 55.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d646-f1" n="42" xml:id="d646-f1-content">
                <p>In telegram 1053, April 5, 6 p.m., from Moscow (<ref target="frus1946v06#pg_732"
                        >vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">vi</hi>, p. 732</ref>), Ambassador Walter
                    Bedell Smith reported a conversation with Generalissimo Stalin who disclaimed
                    any intention to attack Turkey.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
